BUSYBOX(1)

NAME
    busybox - BusyBox multi-call Unix compatibility tools

SYNOPSIS
    busybox applet [arg ...]
    applet [arg ...]

DESCRIPTION
    busybox is the SmallOS build of BusyBox. It provides a broader set of
    familiar Unix applets without replacing native SmallOS tools.

    The SmallOS shell searches /bin, /usr/bin, and /usr/sbin first. If a bare
    command is not found there, it tries /usr/bin/busybox with the original
    command name as the applet. This keeps native commands preferred while
    making applets such as grep, sed, awk, df, du, free, ps, tar, gzip,
    gunzip, od, hexdump, ifconfig, route, ping, readlink, ln, chgrp, md5sum,
    sha1sum, sha256sum, link, mkfifo, mknod, which, expr, and test available.

    /bin/sh is a tiny launcher for BusyBox ash.

SCOPE
    This is a useful Unix compatibility layer, not full Linux emulation.
    ext2-backed hard links, symbolic links, readlink, chmod/chown-style
    metadata, truncate, and stat-visible FIFO/character/block/socket nodes are
    implemented for filesystem applets. Kernel-backed pseudo mount management,
    classic eth0 interface/route ioctls, /proc/net/dev, /proc/net/route, raw
    IPv4 ICMP sockets, UDP DNS resolver traffic, and IPv4/TCP sockets are
    implemented for mount, ifconfig, route, ping, nc, plain HTTP wget, and
    httpd. Init/login/getty behavior, authentication semantics, rtnetlink ip,
    HTTPS/TLS, and complete /dev device behavior remain outside this
    compatibility wave.

FILES
    /usr/bin/busybox
        BusyBox multi-call binary.

    /bin/sh
        Launcher for BusyBox ash.

    /proc
        Virtual process and system status files used by BusyBox applets.

    /dev
        Virtual null, zero, tty, console, and fd entries.

SEE ALSO
    sh(1), shell(1), smallos(7)
